  "question": {
    "text": "Let the foot of perpendicular from a point $\\mathrm{P}(1,2,-1)$ to the straight line $\\mathrm{L}: \\frac{\\mathrm{x}}{1}=\\frac{\\mathrm{y}}{0}=\\frac{\\mathrm{z}}{-1}$ be $N$. Let a line be drawn from $P$ parallel to the plane $x+y+2 z=0$ which meets $L$ at point $Q$. If $\\alpha$ is the acute angle between the lines PN and PQ , then $\\cos \\alpha$ is equal to $\\qquad$"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{3}}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y}{0}=\\frac{z}{-1}=\\lambda$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{N}(\\lambda, 0,-\\lambda)$\n$\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{b}}=\\hat{\\mathrm{i}}-\\hat{\\mathrm{k}}$\n<img src=\"assets\\st0003_6da576057cd6.png\"/>\n$\\because \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{PN}} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{b}}=0$\n$\\Rightarrow 1(1-\\lambda)-(\\lambda-1)=0 \\Rightarrow \\lambda=1$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{N}(1,0,-1)$\n$\\operatorname{Let} \\mathrm{Q}(\\mu, 0,-\\mu)$\n$\\because \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{n}}=\\hat{\\mathrm{i}}+\\hat{\\mathrm{j}}+2 \\hat{\\mathrm{k}}$\nNow,\n<img src=\"assets\\st0003_24b5e0872d57.png\"/>\n$\\because \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{PQ}} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{n}}=0$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mu=-1$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Q}(-1,0,1)$\n$\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{PN}}=2 \\hat{\\mathrm{j}}$ and $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{PQ}}=2 \\hat{i}+2 \\hat{j}-2 \\hat{k}$\n$\\Rightarrow \\cos \\alpha=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$"
  }
